WEBVTT

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:04.146
We also got to allay a certain hidden fear that I didn't realize

00:00:04.146 --> 00:00:07.896
was quite real and was stopping us from doing our own travel

00:00:07.896 --> 00:00:11.646
planning, which is, what's going to happen in the event that

00:00:11.646 --> 00:00:15.581
something happens out there? Typically, when you have an agent,

00:00:15.581 --> 00:00:19.639
they're your fallback plan. What do I do? Where do I go? How do I

00:00:19.639 --> 00:00:23.030
do this? And so, Maria also then showed us how we then

00:00:23.030 --> 00:00:27.188
do the backup planning, mitigate, think through everything, and say,

00:00:27.188 --> 00:00:31.225
what are the do's and don'ts, what are the things that you need to

00:00:31.225 --> 00:00:35.202
have as a backup plan? And that really worked well for us. So, it

00:00:35.202 --> 00:00:36.960
moved us then into a space of

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:41.421
Individualized or independent travel does not mean unsupported

00:00:41.421 --> 00:00:45.812
travel, right? It means being better prepared, and you really

00:00:45.812 --> 00:00:50.627
are able to know where else do you call, or what should you have on

00:00:50.627 --> 00:00:54.380
hand, and then should that happen, what will you do?

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:57.360
You know, the world is huge, and there's

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:01.345
so many hacks coming along that you can tap into, and Maria has a

00:01:01.345 --> 00:01:04.846
wealth of experience. 90-plus countries, I mean, for sure

00:01:04.846 --> 00:01:08.529
there's a thing or two she has definitely uncovered that you

00:01:08.529 --> 00:01:12.454
haven't. And it doesn't have to be scientific, it just has to be

00:01:12.454 --> 00:01:16.499
tried, tested, and practical, and that she is. So, give it a go, I

00:01:16.499 --> 00:01:18.310
would recommend it. Thank you.

